  <h1>Zero-Knowledge Architecture</h1>
  <p style="font-size: 1.15rem; color: #cbd5e1; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
    RenegadeBox is engineered around the principle of strict client-side isolation. The storage provider, network intermediaries, and the WebSocket coordination hub are treated as untrusted Byzantine adversaries.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Threat Model &amp; Assumptions</h2>
  <div class="glass-card" style="padding: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
    <h3 style="margin-top: 0; color: #f43f5e; font-size: 1.1rem;">Adversary Capabilities</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Cloud Storage Provider (AWS, Cloudflare, Backblaze):</strong> Has full access to list, inspect, or modify objects in the S3 bucket. May attempt to index file names, mine documents for training AI models, or sell customer metadata.</li>
      <li><strong>Network Eavesdropper / ISP:</strong> Can monitor all outbound and inbound packets, TLS connections, and payload sizes.</li>
      <li><strong>WebSocket Relay Operator:</strong> Operates the real-time notification hub. Can inspect, drop, or delay any WebSocket frame transmitted across the wire.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 style="color: #34d399; font-size: 1.1rem; margin-top: 1.5rem;">Security Invariants Enforced</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Zero Plaintext Content Leakage:</strong> File payloads are encrypted with AES-256-GCM before leaving host RAM. No plaintext byte is ever written to network sockets.</li>
      <li><strong>Zero Plaintext Metadata Leakage:</strong> File names, paths, directory structures, and file extensions are obfuscated using HMAC-SHA256 derived pseudorandom hashes. S3 and the Hub only see opaque hashes like <code>rb/blobs/b4/b498e820c78a0112...</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Cryptographic Tamper Resistance:</strong> Any byte modification in S3 triggers an AEAD tag mismatch, causing the client engine to reject the compromised blob immediately.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>2. Key Hierarchy &amp; Derivation</h2>
  <p>
    RenegadeBox employs a clean multi-layer cryptographic key schedule to separate concerns and prevent key wear-out:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>User Passphrase:</strong> The user selects a high-entropy passphrase during initial setup.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Key Wrapping Key (KWK):</strong> Derived from the passphrase using <strong>Argon2id</strong> with memory cost parameter <code>m=65536</code> (64 MB), time cost <code>t=3</code> iterations, and parallelism <code>p=4</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Vault Master Key (VMK):</strong> A cryptographically secure 256-bit random key generated by the OS CSPRNG. The VMK is wrapped with the KWK using AES-256-GCM and stored in <code>rb/vault.json</code> in S3.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Subkey Derivation via HKDF-SHA256:</strong> From the Master Key, three cryptographically independent subkeys are derived:
      <ul>
        <li><code>Content Encryption Key (CEK):</code> Encrypts and decrypts 4MB file chunks.</li>
        <li><code>Name Obfuscation Key (NOK):</code> Computes HMAC hashes for file paths and directory trees.</li>
        <li><code>Journal Signature Key (JSK):</code> Authenticates append-only journal mutation records.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>3. 4MB Content-Addressed Chunking</h2>
  <p>
    Files are segmented into deterministic 4MB blocks. Each block is encrypted with AES-256-GCM using a unique 96-bit nonce.
  </p>
  <pre><code>Plaintext Chunk (4MB) 
  → AES-256-GCM Encrypt(CEK, Nonce, Chunk)
  → Ciphertext + 16-byte Poly1305 Tag
  → S3 Object Key: rb/blobs/&lt;prefix&gt;/&lt;hash&gt;</code></pre>
  <p>
    Content addressing enables automatic deduplication: if two files share identical blocks (or a large file undergoes a minor edit), only the new or modified 4MB chunks are uploaded. Unaltered chunks are reused without re-uploading.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Per-Device Append-Only Journals</h2>
  <p>
    Traditional cloud sync tools rely on centralized database locks. In a decentralized, multi-device environment, locks create single points of failure and race conditions.
  </p>
  <p>
    RenegadeBox solves this by granting each paired device its own immutable, append-only journal in S3:
  </p>
  <pre><code>s3://&lt;bucket&gt;/rb/journal/&lt;device-uuid-1&gt;/00000000000000000001
s3://&lt;bucket&gt;/rb/journal/&lt;device-uuid-1&gt;/00000000000000000002
s3://&lt;bucket&gt;/rb/journal/&lt;device-uuid-2&gt;/00000000000000000001</code></pre>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Conditional S3 PUTs:</strong> Every journal sequence file is committed using <code>If-None-Match: *</code> to prevent accidental overwrites.</li>
    <li><strong>Ransomware Resilience:</strong> Because journals are append-only and past entries are never mutated, any malicious encryption or accidental deletion can be rolled back to any historical point in time.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>5. Deterministic Conflict Resolution</h2>
  <p>
    When two machines edit the same file simultaneously while disconnected from the network, RenegadeBox resolves the conflict deterministically upon reconnecting:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>The version with the higher Lamport clock timestamp is promoted to the canonical file name.</li>
    <li>The alternative version is preserved without data loss as:
      <br />
      <code>filename (conflicted copy device-b 2026-10-15).ext</code>
    </li>
    <li>Both versions are indexed and synced to all devices, leaving the user in complete control.</li>
  </ul>
